        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          STS-83 was the 22nd flight of the Space Shuttle Columbia and the 83rd flight of the Space Shuttle system.  The orbiter crew included Commander James D. Halsell, Susan L. Still, Payload Commander Janice E. Voss, Mission Specialists Donald A. Thomas and Michael L. Gernhardt, and Payload Specialists Roger Crouch and Greg Linter.  The STS-83 mission was scheduled to launch on April 3, 1997.  However, a decision was made to postpone the launch by one day after a water coolant line in the orbiter's payload bay was found not properly insulated.  Additional insulation was required to prevent this line from possibly freezing during Columbia's scheduled 16 days in space.  STS-83 launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on April 4, 1997.<P>The primary objective for the STS-83 mission was to conduct experiments and evaluate facilities associated with the Microgravity Science Laboratory-1 (MSL-1) payload.  Crewmembers on MSL-1 tested hardware, facilities and procedures to be used on the International Space Station (ISS).  New procedures designed to place scientific payloads into orbit and new methods of integrating experiments and equipment were tested.  Scientists from four space agencies developed 33 investigations for the MSL-1 mission.  Representatives of the European Space Agency, the German Space Agency and the National Space Development Agency of Japan joined NASA and scientists from across the United States in this research mission.<P>Three Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs) were performed on this mission (Immunological Assessment of Crewmembers, Monitoring Latent Virus Reactivation and Shedding in Astronauts and Integration of the Space Shuttle Launch and Entry Suit). A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who serve as the test subjects. These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage. Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.<P>Three Development Test Objectives (DTO) were also performed on this mission (Evaluation of Microbial Capture Device in Microgravity, Radiation Measurements in Shuttle Crew Compartment and Cabin Air Monitoring). A DTO is a NASA-sponsored investigation that is performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers to evaluate new hardware and procedures involving the Orbiter, its subsystems, and its support equipment.<P> The Space Shuttle Columbia landed at Kennedy Space Center in Florida on April 8, 1997.  The flight was originally scheduled to last 15 days and land at KSC on April 19, but the mission was cut short due to problems with one of Columbia's fuel cells.  The MSL payload was re-flown on STS-94 in July, 1997.
        </div>

